{
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The 5.00 SGD is already off the rider's side of the ledger, and the driver's filing is an attempt to put it back. The payments ledger records refund_issued_sgd 5.00 with payment_status REFUNDED and a single cancellation-fee line reconciled to the total charged (EV-FARE-05067), and the trip record carries the platform's fee_reversed event at 2026-09-13T10:46:10, 'refunded to rider and withheld from driver pending review' (EV-TRIP-05067). POL-GEN-03 defines NO_ACTION as the filing party's claim not being supported and the present state standing, and it is party-relative and 'never means restore a charge'. The driver is the filer here, so the 5.00 can move back to him only if his own claim is affirmatively made out; unless it is, the refund already issued to the rider stands and the amounts on the ruling are null.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-FARE-05067", "EV-TRIP-05067"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-GEN-03"],
      "relies_on": "fare_breakdown.refund_issued_sgd / fare_breakdown.payment_status / trip_record.events[fee_reversed]",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "This trip is on-demand and the record fixes no scheduled pickup time at all: scheduled_at is null and the derived driver_deadline is null, with the stated reason 'on-demand trip: no scheduled time' (EV-TRIP-05067). POL-NS-24's test is the scheduled pickup time plus 5 minutes, so on this record that deadline cannot be fixed, and POL-NS-24's own reading of v2.1 sends the case to POL-GEN-26 where the deadline cannot be fixed. POL-GEN-26 requires the case to be escalated rather than an outcome inferred, and POL-GEN-03 makes an escalation terminal and holds the amount — so the 5.00 stays credited to the rider and withheld from the driver instead of being released to him as compensation.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-05067"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-NS-24", "POL-GEN-26", "POL-GEN-03"],
      "relies_on": "trip_record.derived.v7.driver_deadline.deadline",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The charge POL-NS-22 authorises is a no-show fee, and this record does not show the rider failing to appear. She read every message the driver sent — MSG-05067-001, -002 and -003 read at 09:22:42, 09:25:37 and 09:29:09, each within about a minute of sending (EV-CHAT-05067) — so she was on her phone and available throughout the free wait POL-NS-21 gives her to come out and board, and her account on file is that she was downstairs waiting. The GPS artifact carries no rider track at all (rider_track empty; the only trace is the driver's own device, EV-GPS-05067), so nothing in the record contradicts her account. The no-show premise on which the driver would take the 5.00 as compensation is not made out on this evidence, and the refund already issued should stand.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-CHAT-05067", "EV-GPS-05067"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-NS-22", "POL-NS-21"],
      "relies_on": "chat_log.messages[].read_at / gps_trace.rider_track",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "Nothing on a profile can decide this trip. POL-GEN-22 confines a fraud flag to raising the confidence an AI ruling needs — it never decides the trip — and POL-GEN-01 forbids every other profile field from establishing a fact about this trip. The rider's snapshot carries fraud_flags 3 (EV-RPROF-05067), so the required confidence for any ruling here is 0.95 whatever the dispute value (POL-GEN-22, carried into no-shows by POL-NS-26), and the driver's claim to take back an already-refunded 5.00 must clear that bar; the rider's rating, cancellation history and prior disputes are context only and cannot be used to reimpose the fee.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-RPROF-05067"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-GEN-22", "POL-GEN-01", "POL-NS-26"],
      "relies_on": "rider_profile.fraud_flags",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "DRIVER_COMPENSATION",
    "amount_sgd": 5.0,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": 5.0,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.96,
    "explanation": "Both sides' claims are answered below.\n\nTHE NO-SHOW FEE WAS CORRECTLY CHARGED, SO THE DRIVER IS OWED THE 5.00 SGD. On this on-demand booking there is no scheduled pickup time, so under POL-NS-21 the 300 s free wait ran from the driver's arrival. The driver auto-arrived at Block 680 at 09:21:45, corroborated by his own GPS trace: the nearest fix is 1.81 m from the pickup point, far inside the 50 m STANDARD arrival radius, and the trace reports the vehicle stationary at 0 kph from 09:21:50 through 09:29:50. The free wait expired at 09:26:45 and the driver did not cancel until 09:30:10 — 505 s after arrival. Before cancelling he sent three in-app messages (09:22:00, 09:25:30, 09:28:40) and placed two in-app calls (09:24:10 and 09:27:20, both unanswered), all before the 09:30:10 cancellation, so POL-NS-23's contact obligation is satisfied on both limbs and nothing there voids the fee. The fare breakdown records a single cancellation-fee line of 5.00 SGD, matching the 5.00 SGD policy fee, so POL-NS-22 applies and the fee stands.\n\nTHE RIDER'S ACCOUNT DOES NOT DEFEAT THE ARRIVAL TEST. The rider says the driver never came to her block and that she was downstairs waiting. That statement is not corroborated — the GPS artifact carries no rider track at all — and it does not displace the POL-NS-21 arrival test, which the trip record and the driver's trace satisfy. She read each message within about a minute, but the record shows she did not board during the free wait, and the no-show fee POL-NS-22 authorises does not depend on anything beyond the free wait and the contact obligation.\n\nTHE RIDER'S ESCALATION ARGUMENT IS REJECTED. The rider argues that POL-NS-24's deadline (scheduled pickup time plus 5 minutes) cannot be fixed on an on-demand trip and that the case must therefore go to POL-GEN-26. POL-NS-24 protects the rider where the DRIVER is late on a scheduled trip; the driver here arrived promptly and the rider did not cancel, so that clause's route is not engaged. POL-NS-21 expressly fixes the free wait from arrival for an on-demand trip, so the deadline is determinable, and no fact the no-show rules need is missing: the arrival, the length of the wait, the messages and calls before cancellation, and the fee charged are all carried by COMPLETE artifacts at weight_multiplier 1.0. The one MISSING artifact, the traffic advisory EV-ADVIS-05067-01 (weight 0.0, issue MISSING_ARTIFACT), bears on no element of POL-NS-21, POL-NS-22 or POL-NS-23, so its absence opens no question and grounds no escalation.\n\nTHE INTERIM REVERSAL IS NOT A FINDING. The 10:46:10 fee_reversed entry is the platform's interim measure after the rider's complaint — 'refunded to rider and withheld from driver pending review' — not a decision on the merits. Because the fee stands on the evidence, the rider's 5.00 SGD refund does not stand, and under POL-NS-22 with POL-GEN-03 the ruling is DRIVER_COMPENSATION, with amount_sgd and driver_compensation_sgd both carrying the 5.00 SGD the ruling moves.\n\nCONFIDENCE. The rider's profile carries fraud_flags 3, so POL-GEN-22 (carried into no-shows by POL-NS-26) requires confidence of 0.95. The deciding facts rest on the trip record, the GPS trace, the chat log and the fare breakdown, all graded COMPLETE at weight_multiplier 1.0, so the cap is 1.0 and the bar is met at 0.96.\n\nREFERRALS. None fires. The fee charged (5.00 SGD) equals the 5.00 SGD policy fee, so POL-NS-22's fee-amount referral does not apply; the driver did arrive, so POL-NS-24's 5.00 SGD goodwill credit (which needs a failed arrival AND a failed contact attempt) does not arise; and the rider's no_show_cancellations_30d of 1, counting this trip, is 2, below POL-NS-25's threshold of 3."
  },
